    "category": "Functions",
    "question_hi": "Python mein keyword-only arguments kya hote hain?",
    "answer_hi": "**Keyword-only arguments are function parameters that must be passed using their parameter name. They cannot be passed by position. They are created by placing `*` before the keyword-only parameters in the function definition.**\n\nExample:\n\n```python\ndef greet(name, *, age):\n    print(name, age)\n\ngreet(\"Vikram\", age=22)   # ✅ Correct\n# greet(\"Vikram\", 22)     # ❌ Error\n```\n\n**Easy way to remember:**\n\n- Positional argument: `greet(\"Vikram\", 22)`\n- Keyword-only argument: `greet(\"Vikram\", age=22)`\n\n**Rule:** After `*`, all parameters become keyword-only and must be passed using `parameter=value`.",
    "question_en": "What are keyword-only arguments in Python?",
    "answer_en": "**Keyword-only arguments are function parameters that must be passed using their parameter name. They cannot be passed by position. They are created by placing `*` before the keyword-only parameters in the function definition.**\n\nExample:\n\n```python\ndef greet(name, *, age):\n    print(name, age)\n\ngreet(\"Vikram\", age=22)   # ✅ Correct\n# greet(\"Vikram\", 22)     # ❌ Error\n```\n\n**Easy way to remember:**\n\n- Positional argument: `greet(\"Vikram\", 22)`\n- Keyword-only argument: `greet(\"Vikram\", age=22)`\n\n**Rule:** After `*`, all parameters become keyword-only and must be passed using `parameter=value`.",
