    "category": "Functions",
    "question_hi": "Kya Python function overloading support karta hai?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Nahi, Python Java ya C++ ki tarah traditional function overloading support nahi karta. Agar hum same naam ke multiple functions define karte hain, toh latest definition pichli ko replace kar deti hai. Similar behavior default arguments, `*args`, ya `kwargs` use karke achieve kiya ja sakta hai.**\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef show():\r\n    print(\"First\")\r\n\r\ndef show():\r\n    print(\"Second\")\r\n\r\nshow()\r\n# Output: Second\r\n```\r\n\r\nPehla `show()` function dusre se overwrite ho jaata hai.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Nahi, Python traditional function overloading support nahi karta. Agar multiple functions ka same naam hai, toh last wala previous ones ko overwrite kar deta hai. Hum default arguments ya `*args` use karke similar behavior achieve kar sakte hain.\r\n\r\n💡 Example:\n\n```python\ndef add(a, b=0, c=0):\n    return a + b + c\n\nprint(add(5))        # 5\nprint(add(5, 10))    # 15\nprint(add(5, 10, 2)) # 17\n```\n\n**Another way:**\n\n```python\ndef add(*args):\n    return sum(args)\n\nprint(add(5))          # 5\nprint(add(5, 10))      # 15\nprint(add(5, 10, 2))   # 17\n```\n\n**Interview Tip:** Python does not have true method overloading like Java or C++. We usually use default arguments or `*args` to achieve similar behavior.",
    "question_en": "Does Python support function overloading?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> No, Python does not support traditional function overloading like Java or C++. If we define multiple functions with the same name, the latest definition replaces the previous one. Similar behavior can be achieved using default arguments, `*args`, or `kwargs`.**\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef show():\r\n    print(\"First\")\r\n\r\ndef show():\r\n    print(\"Second\")\r\n\r\nshow()\r\n# Output: Second\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first `show()` function is overwritten by the second one.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> No, Python does not support traditional function overloading. If multiple functions have the same name, the last one overwrites the previous ones. We can achieve similar behavior using default arguments or `*args`.\r\n\r\n💡 *Example:\n\n```python\ndef add(a, b=0, c=0):\n    return a + b + c\n\nprint(add(5))        # 5\nprint(add(5, 10))    # 15\nprint(add(5, 10, 2)) # 17\n```\n\n**Another way:**\n\n```python\ndef add(*args):\n    return sum(args)\n\nprint(add(5))          # 5\nprint(add(5, 10))      # 15\nprint(add(5, 10, 2))   # 17\n```\n\n**Interview Tip:** Python does not have true method overloading like Java or C++. We usually use default arguments or `*args` to achieve similar behavior.",
    "source": "doc",
    "topic": "python",
