    "category": "Lists & Tuples",
    "question_hi": "List se duplicates kaise remove karte hain?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> List se duplicates remove karne ke do common tareeke hain. Hum list ko `set` mein convert karke wapas list mein convert kar sakte hain, lekin isse elements ka order change ho sakta hai. Agar humein original order maintain karna hai, toh hum `dict.fromkeys()` use kar sakte hain.\r\n\r\n**Example 1 (`set()` use karke):**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nnumbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1]\r\n\r\nunique = list(set(numbers))\r\n\r\nprint(unique)\r\n# Output: [1, 2, 3]   # Order vary ho sakta hai\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Example 2 (Order Preserve karte hue):**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nnumbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1]\r\n\r\nunique = list(dict.fromkeys(numbers))\r\n\r\nprint(unique)\r\n# Output: [1, 2, 3]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Hum list ko `set` mein convert karke duplicates remove kar sakte hain. Agar humein original order preserve karna ho, toh hum `dict.fromkeys()` use kar sakte hain.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **`set()` → Duplicates remove karta hai, lekin order guaranteed nahi hai.**\r\n* **`dict.fromkeys()` → Duplicates remove karta hai aur original order preserve karta hai.**",
    "question_en": "How do you remove duplicates from a list?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> There are two common ways to remove duplicates from a list. We can convert the list to a `set` and then back to a list, but this may change the order of the elements. If we want to keep the original order, we can use `dict.fromkeys()`.\r\n\r\n**Example 1 (Using `set()`):**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nnumbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1]\r\n\r\nunique = list(set(numbers))\r\n\r\nprint(unique)\r\n# Output: [1, 2, 3]   # Order may vary\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Example 2 (Preserving Order):**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nnumbers = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1]\r\n\r\nunique = list(dict.fromkeys(numbers))\r\n\r\nprint(unique)\r\n# Output: [1, 2, 3]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> We can remove duplicates by converting the list to a `set`. If we need to preserve the original order, we can use `dict.fromkeys()`.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **`set()` → Removes duplicates, but order is not guaranteed.**\r\n* **`dict.fromkeys()` → Removes duplicates and preserves the original order.**",
    "source": "doc",
    "topic": "python",
    "num": "48"
