    "category": "Dicts & Sets",
    "question_hi": "Kya dictionary keys koi bhi data type ho sakti hain?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Dictionary keys koi bhi hashable aur immutable data type ho sakti hain. Common examples strings, numbers, aur tuples hain. List, set, aur dictionary jaise mutable data types ko dictionary keys ke roop mein use nahi kiya ja sakta kyunki inki values change ho sakti hain.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"7y6w7u\"\r\n# Valid dictionary keys\r\ndata = {\r\n    \"name\": \"Vikram\",\r\n    1: \"one\",\r\n    (1, 2): \"tuple key\"\r\n}\r\n\r\nprint(data)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python id=\"n9c4au\"\r\n# Invalid dictionary key\r\ndata = {\r\n    [1, 2]: \"list key\"\r\n}\r\n\r\n# Error: TypeError (unhashable type: 'list')\r\n```\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Dictionary keys immutable aur hashable honi chahiye. Strings, numbers, aur tuples keys ke roop mein use ho sakte hain, lekin lists, sets, aur dictionaries keys ke roop mein use nahi ho sakte kyunki wo mutable hote hain.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* ✅ **Allowed:** `str`, `int`, `float`, `tuple`\r\n* ❌ **Not allowed:** `list`, `set`, `dict`\r\n* **Rule: Key creation ke baad change nahi honi chahiye**",
    "question_en": "Can dictionary keys be any data type?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Dictionary keys can be any data type that is hashable and immutable. Common examples are strings, numbers, and tuples. Mutable data types like lists, sets, and dictionaries cannot be used as dictionary keys because their values can change.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"7y6w7u\"\r\n# Valid dictionary keys\r\ndata = {\r\n    \"name\": \"Vikram\",\r\n    1: \"one\",\r\n    (1, 2): \"tuple key\"\r\n}\r\n\r\nprint(data)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python id=\"n9c4au\"\r\n# Invalid dictionary key\r\ndata = {\r\n    [1, 2]: \"list key\"\r\n}\r\n\r\n# Error: TypeError (unhashable type: 'list')\r\n```\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Dictionary keys must be immutable and hashable. Strings, numbers, and tuples can be used as keys, but lists, sets, and dictionaries cannot be used as keys because they are mutable.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* ✅ **Allowed:** `str`, `int`, `float`, `tuple`\r\n* ❌ **Not allowed:** `list`, `set`, `dict`\r\n* **Rule: Key should not change after creation**",
