    "category": "OOP",
    "question_hi": "OOP ke chaar pillars kya hain?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) ke chaar main pillars hain Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, aur Abstraction. Ye concepts humein classes aur objects use karke code organize karne mein madad karte hain, jisse ye reusable, secure, aur maintain karna easier ban jaata hai.\r\n\r\n### **1. Encapsulation**\r\n\r\n> Encapsulation ka matlab hai data aur methods ko ek class ke andar saath mein wrap karna aur data tak access control karna.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"p2z7n8\"\r\nclass Bank:\r\n    def __init__(self, balance):\r\n        self.__balance = balance\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **2. Inheritance**\r\n\r\n> Inheritance ek class ko dusri class ki properties aur methods use karne deta hai. Ye code reuse mein madad karta hai.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"5v9xq1\"\r\nclass Animal:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Sound\")\r\n\r\nclass Dog(Animal):\r\n    pass\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **3. Polymorphism**\r\n\r\n> Polymorphism ka matlab hai ki same method ya operation object ke basis par different behave kar sakta hai.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"6kq2rm\"\r\nclass Dog:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Bark\")\r\n\r\nclass Cat:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Meow\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **4. Abstraction**\r\n\r\n> Abstraction ka matlab hai internal implementation details ko hide karna aur user ko sirf necessary information dikhana.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n* Car chalana → Aap steering aur pedals use karte hain bina internal engine working jaane.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (30 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> OOP ke chaar pillars hain Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, aur Abstraction. Encapsulation data ko protect karta hai, Inheritance code reuse provide karta hai, Polymorphism same interface use karke different behaviors allow allow karta hai, aur Abstraction unnecessary details hide karta hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **E → Encapsulation → Data hiding**\r\n* **I → Inheritance → Code reuse**\r\n* **P → Polymorphism → Many forms**\r\n* **A → Abstraction → Complexity hide karo**",
    "question_en": "What are the four pillars of OOP?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> The four main pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) are Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction. These concepts help us organize code using classes and objects, making it reusable, secure, and easier to maintain.\r\n\r\n### **1. Encapsulation**\r\n\r\n> Encapsulation means wrapping data and methods together inside a class and controlling access to the data.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"p2z7n8\"\r\nclass Bank:\r\n    def __init__(self, balance):\r\n        self.__balance = balance\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **2. Inheritance**\r\n\r\n> Inheritance allows one class to use the properties and methods of another class. It helps in code reuse.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"5v9xq1\"\r\nclass Animal:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Sound\")\r\n\r\nclass Dog(Animal):\r\n    pass\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **3. Polymorphism**\r\n\r\n> Polymorphism means the same method or operation can behave differently depending on the object.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python id=\"6kq2rm\"\r\nclass Dog:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Bark\")\r\n\r\nclass Cat:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        print(\"Meow\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **4. Abstraction**\r\n\r\n> Abstraction means hiding internal implementation details and showing only the necessary information to the user.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n* Driving a car → You use the steering and pedals without knowing the internal engine working.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (30 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> The four pillars of OOP are Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction. Encapsulation protects data, Inheritance provides code reuse, Polymorphism allows different behaviors using the same interface, and Abstraction hides unnecessary details.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **E → Encapsulation → Data hiding**\r\n* **I → Inheritance → Code reuse**\r\n* **P → Polymorphism → Many forms**\r\n* **A → Abstraction → Hide complexity**",
    "source": "doc",
    "topic": "python",
    "num": "63"
