    "category": "OOP",
    "question_hi": "Class mein self kya hai?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> `self` ek class ke current object (instance) ka reference hai. Ye automatically instance methods ko first argument ke roop mein pass hota hai aur object ke attributes aur methods ko access ya modify karne ke liye use hota hai.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"2q8x4v\"\r\nclass Student:\r\n    def __init__(self, name):\r\n        self.name = name\r\n\r\n    def show(self):\r\n        print(self.name)\r\n\r\nstudent1 = Student(\"Vikram\")\r\n\r\nstudent1.show()\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text id=\"9z4p6s\"\r\nVikram\r\n```\r\n\r\nYahan:\r\n\r\n* `self.name` current object ke `name` attribute ko refer karta hai.\r\n* `student1` wo object hai jo `show()` method call kar raha hai, isliye `self`, `student1` ko refer karta hai.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> `self` class ke current object ko refer karta hai. Isse instance methods ke andar object ke attributes aur methods ko access aur modify karne ke liye use kiya jaata hai. Jab hum object ke through method call karte hain toh Python automatically ise pass karta hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **self = current object**\r\n* **self.attribute → Object data access karo**\r\n* **self.method() → Object method call karo** ✅",
    "question_en": "What is self in a class?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> `self` is a reference to the current object (instance) of a class. It is automatically passed as the first argument to instance methods and is used to access or modify the object's attributes and methods.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"2q8x4v\"\r\nclass Student:\r\n    def __init__(self, name):\r\n        self.name = name\r\n\r\n    def show(self):\r\n        print(self.name)\r\n\r\nstudent1 = Student(\"Vikram\")\r\n\r\nstudent1.show()\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text id=\"9z4p6s\"\r\nVikram\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere:\r\n\r\n* `self.name` refers to the `name` attribute of the current object.\r\n* `student1` is the object calling the `show()` method, so `self` refers to `student1`.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> `self` refers to the current object of a class. It is used inside instance methods to access and modify the object's attributes and methods. Python automatically passes it when we call a method through an object.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **self = current object**\r\n* **self.attribute → Access object data**\r\n* **self.method() → Call object method** ✅",
