    "category": "File Handling",
    "question_hi": "'r+', 'w+', aur 'a+' file modes mein kya difference hai?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> `r+`, `w+`, aur `a+` modes file mein reading aur writing dono allow karte hain, lekin ye existing data ko differently handle karte hain.\r\n\r\n### **1. `r+` (Read + Write)**\r\n\r\n> Ek existing file ko reading aur writing ke liye open karta hai. Ye existing content delete nahi karta.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"r+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* File already exist honi chahiye\r\n* Existing data rehta hai\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **2. `w+` (Write + Read)**\r\n\r\n> Ek file ko reading aur writing ke liye open karta hai, lekin ye pehle saara existing content remove kar deta hai. Agar file exist nahi karti, toh ye ek nayi file create karta hai.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"w+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* Old data delete ho jaata hai ❌\r\n* New content likha ja sakta hai\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **3. `a+` (Append + Read)**\r\n\r\n> Ek file ko reading aur writing ke liye open karta hai, lekin naya data existing content delete kiye bina file ke end mein add hota hai.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"a+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* Existing data rehta hai ✅\r\n* Naya data end mein add hota hai\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (30 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> `r+` existing data remove kiye bina reading aur writing allow karta hai. `w+` reading aur writing allow karta hai lekin pehle file clear kar deta hai. `a+` reading aur writing allow karta hai jabki naya data file ke end mein add hota hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n| Mode | Read | Write | Old Data            |\r\n| ---- | ---- | ----- | ------------------- |\r\n| `r+` | ✅    | ✅     | Rakhta hai               |\r\n| `w+` | ✅    | ✅     | Delete karta hai             |\r\n| `a+` | ✅    | ✅     | Rakhta hai + End mein add karta hai |\r\n\r\n✅ **`+` ka matlab hai read aur write dono**\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nQ.File handling ke liye with statement kyu use kiya jaata hai?\r\n\r\nA. **Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> `with` statement file handling mein isliye use kiya jaata hai kyunki ye file resource ko automatically manage karta hai. Ye block complete hone ke baad file ko automatically close kar deta hai, chahe error aaye ya nahi. Ye resource leaks prevent karne mein madad karta hai aur code ko cleaner banata hai.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"8c1z7m\"\r\nwith open(\"data.txt\", \"r\") as file:\r\n    content = file.read()\r\n\r\nprint(content)\r\n```\r\n\r\nYahan:\r\n\r\n* `with` block mein enter karte hi file open hoti hai.\r\n* Block se bahar nikalte hi file automatically close ho jaati hai.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> `with` statement ka use files ko automatically open aur close karne ke liye kiya jaata hai. Ye ensure karta hai ki file properly close ho, chahe koi exception aaye, isliye ye Python mein file handling ka recommended tarika hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n`with` ke bina:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfile = open(\"data.txt\", \"r\")\r\nfile.close()\r\n```\r\n\r\n`with` ke saath:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"data.txt\", \"r\") as file:\r\n    # file ke saath kaam karo\r\n```\r\n\r\n✅ **`with` = Automatic cleanup**",
    "question_en": "What is the difference between 'r+', 'w+', and 'a+' file modes?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> `r+`, `w+`, and `a+` modes allow both reading and writing in a file, but they handle existing data differently.\r\n\r\n### **1. `r+` (Read + Write)**\r\n\r\n> Opens an existing file for reading and writing. It does not delete existing content.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"r+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* File must already exist\r\n* Existing data remains\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **2. `w+` (Write + Read)**\r\n\r\n> Opens a file for reading and writing, but it removes all existing content first. If the file does not exist, it creates a new file.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"w+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* Old data is deleted ❌\r\n* New content can be written\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **3. `a+` (Append + Read)**\r\n\r\n> Opens a file for reading and writing, but new data is added at the end of the file without deleting existing content.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nopen(\"file.txt\", \"a+\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n* Existing data remains ✅\r\n* New data is added at the end\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (30 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> `r+` allows reading and writing without removing existing data. `w+` allows reading and writing but clears the file first. `a+` allows reading and writing while adding new data at the end of the file.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n| Mode | Read | Write | Old Data            |\r\n| ---- | ---- | ----- | ------------------- |\r\n| `r+` | ✅    | ✅     | Keeps               |\r\n| `w+` | ✅    | ✅     | Deletes             |\r\n| `a+` | ✅    | ✅     | Keeps + Adds at end |\r\n\r\n✅ **`+` means both read and write**\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nQ.Why is the with statement used for file handling?\r\n\r\nA. **Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> The `with` statement is used in file handling because it automatically manages the file resource. It closes the file automatically after the block is completed, even if an error occurs. It helps prevent resource leaks and makes the code cleaner.\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"8c1z7m\"\r\nwith open(\"data.txt\", \"r\") as file:\r\n    content = file.read()\r\n\r\nprint(content)\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere:\r\n\r\n* File opens when entering the `with` block.\r\n* File automatically closes when leaving the block.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> The `with` statement is used to automatically open and close files. It ensures the file is properly closed even if an exception occurs, so it is the recommended way for file handling in Python.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\nWithout `with`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfile = open(\"data.txt\", \"r\")\r\nfile.close()\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith `with`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"data.txt\", \"r\") as file:\r\n    # work with file\r\n```\r\n\r\n✅ **`with` = Automatic cleanup**",
