    "category": "Advanced",
    "question_hi": "Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) kya hai?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> GIL (Global Interpreter Lock) CPython mein ek lock hai jo ek time par sirf ek thread ko Python bytecode execute karne deta hai. Ye multiple threads ko ek saath Python objects modify karne se rokta hai, lekin ye CPU-intensive tasks ke liye true parallel execution ko limit karta hai.\r\n\r\n### **Python GIL kyu use karta hai?**\r\n\r\n> GIL memory ko safely manage karne mein madad karta hai kyunki Python ka memory management system completely thread-safe nahi hai. Ye multiple threads ko ek saath Python objects access karne aur data corruption cause karne se rokta hai.\r\n\r\n### **Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"k8m2vz\"\r\nimport threading\r\n\r\ndef task():\r\n    for i in range(1000000):\r\n        pass\r\n\r\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=task)\r\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=task)\r\n\r\nt1.start()\r\nt2.start()\r\n```\r\n\r\nChahe do threads create kiye gaye hon, CPython mein GIL ki wajah se ek time par sirf ek thread Python code execute karta hai.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Global Interpreter Lock CPython mein ek mechanism hai jo ek time par sirf ek thread ko Python bytecode execute karne deta hai. Ye memory safety improve karta hai lekin CPU-bound multithreading tasks ke liye full parallelism prevent karta hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **GIL = Ek time par ek Python thread run hota hai**\r\n* **CPU-bound tasks → GIL threading ko slow kar sakta hai**\r\n* **I/O-bound tasks → Threading phir bhi achhe se kaam karti hai**\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n* Files download karna 🌐 → Threading madad karti hai ✅\r\n* Heavy calculations 🧮 → Iske bajaye multiprocessing use karein ✅",
    "question_en": "What is the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> GIL (Global Interpreter Lock) is a lock in CPython that allows only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time. It prevents multiple threads from modifying Python objects simultaneously, but it limits true parallel execution for CPU-intensive tasks.\r\n\r\n### **Why does Python use GIL?**\r\n\r\n> The GIL helps manage memory safely because Python's memory management system is not completely thread-safe. It prevents multiple threads from accessing Python objects at the same time and causing data corruption.\r\n\r\n### **Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"k8m2vz\"\r\nimport threading\r\n\r\ndef task():\r\n    for i in range(1000000):\r\n        pass\r\n\r\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=task)\r\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=task)\r\n\r\nt1.start()\r\nt2.start()\r\n```\r\n\r\nEven though two threads are created, in CPython only one thread executes Python code at a time because of the GIL.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> The Global Interpreter Lock is a mechanism in CPython that allows only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time. It improves memory safety but prevents full parallelism for CPU-bound multithreading tasks.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n* **GIL = One Python thread runs at a time**\r\n* **CPU-bound tasks → GIL can slow down threading**\r\n* **I/O-bound tasks → Threading still works well**\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n* Downloading files 🌐 → Threading helps ✅\r\n* Heavy calculations 🧮 → Use multiprocessing instead ✅",
    "source": "doc",
    "topic": "python",
    "num": "98"
