    "question_hi": "Monkey patching kya hai?",
    "answer_hi": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Monkey patching ek class, object, ya module ke behavior ko runtime par uske original source code ko modify kiye bina change ya add karne ka process hai. Isse commonly testing mein real functions ya objects ko mock versions se replace karne ke liye use kiya jaata hai.\r\n\r\n### **Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"n7x2mq\"\r\nclass Dog:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        return \"Bark\"\r\n\r\ndef new_sound(self):\r\n    return \"New Bark\"\r\n\r\nDog.sound = new_sound   # Monkey patching\r\n\r\ndog = Dog()\r\n\r\nprint(dog.sound())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text id=\"8p4mcz\"\r\nNew Bark\r\n```\r\n\r\nYahan:\r\n\r\n* Original `sound()` method runtime par replace kiya gaya.\r\n* Class ka source code change nahi hua.\r\n\r\n### **Common Use Case:**\r\n\r\n**Testing:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"q6v9sa\"\r\n# Testing ke dauraan real API call ko fake response se replace karo\r\napi.get_data = mock_get_data\r\n```\r\n\r\nIsse actual API call kiye bina testing possible hoti hai.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Monkey patching ka matlab hai runtime par ek class ya module ka behavior dynamically modify karna bina uska original code change kiye. Isse often testing mein dependencies ko mock implementations se replace karne ke liye use kiya jaata hai.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n**Monkey patch = Creation ke baad behavior change karo** 🐒\r\n\r\n* Runtime modification ✅\r\n* Source code change nahi ✅\r\n* Testing/mocking mein common ✅",
    "question_en": "What is monkey patching?",
    "answer_en": "**Interview Answer (Simple English):**\r\n\r\n> Monkey patching is the process of changing or adding behavior to a class, object, or module at runtime without modifying its original source code. It is commonly used in testing to replace real functions or objects with mock versions.\r\n\r\n### **Example:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"n7x2mq\"\r\nclass Dog:\r\n    def sound(self):\r\n        return \"Bark\"\r\n\r\ndef new_sound(self):\r\n    return \"New Bark\"\r\n\r\nDog.sound = new_sound   # Monkey patching\r\n\r\ndog = Dog()\r\n\r\nprint(dog.sound())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text id=\"8p4mcz\"\r\nNew Bark\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere:\r\n\r\n* Original `sound()` method was replaced at runtime.\r\n* The class source code was not changed.\r\n\r\n### **Common Use Case:**\r\n\r\n**Testing:**\r\n\r\n```python id=\"q6v9sa\"\r\n# Replace real API call with fake response during testing\r\napi.get_data = mock_get_data\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis allows testing without calling the actual API.\r\n\r\n### **Short Version (20 seconds):**\r\n\r\n> Monkey patching means modifying a class or module's behavior dynamically at runtime without changing its original code. It is often used in testing to replace dependencies with mock implementations.\r\n\r\n💡 **Easy way to remember:**\r\n\r\n**Monkey patch = Change behavior after creation 🐒**\r\n\r\n* Runtime modification ✅\r\n* No source code change ✅\r\n* Common in testing/mocking ✅",
